from (values
('127.409.d.12.A',3,'A paperclip bent slightly springs back, but bent far enough it stays bent. Staying bent is called:','["fracture only", "elastic deformation", "plastic deformation", "expansion"]'::jsonb,2,'Plastic deformation is permanent.','original'),
('127.409.d.12.A',4,'Elastic deformation is defined as deformation that:','["needs heat", "is permanent", "causes fracture", "disappears when the load is removed"]'::jsonb,3,'Elastic means recoverable.','original'),
('127.409.d.12.B',3,'A simply supported beam with a center load deflects much more if its length is increased, because deflection grows rapidly with:','["span length", "paint", "label", "color"]'::jsonb,0,'Deflection depends on length cubed.','original'),
('127.409.d.12.B',4,'A stiffer material, with a larger modulus of elasticity E, causes the same beam to deflect:','["randomly", "less", "infinitely", "more"]'::jsonb,1,'Deflection is inversely proportional to E.','original'),
('127.409.d.12.C',3,'A beam fails at 12,000 N and is designed to carry 4,000 N. The factor of safety is:','["a factor of 8", "a factor of 16,000", "a factor of 3", "a factor of 0.33"]'::jsonb,2,'12,000 / 4,000 = 3.','original'),
('127.409.d.12.C',4,'A factor of safety less than 1 means the design is expected to:','["be too heavy", "cost less", "last forever", "fail under the design load"]'::jsonb,3,'The strength is below the load.','original'),
('127.409.d.12.D',3,'A long, slender column is more likely than a short, thick one to fail by:','["buckling", "melting", "dissolving", "rusting"]'::jsonb,0,'Slender columns buckle under compression.','original'),
('127.409.d.12.D',4,'Increasing a bar''s cross-sectional area, with the same load, makes the stress:','["infinite", "lower", "higher", "unchanged"]'::jsonb,1,'Stress = force / area.','original'),
('127.409.d.12.E',3,'A tapered wind-turbine tower is thicker at the base because the bending moment there is:','["zero", "smallest", "greatest", "imaginary"]'::jsonb,2,'Moments grow toward the base.','original'),
('127.409.d.12.E',4,'Tapering a cantilever beam saves material by making it thinner where the bending moment is:','["fixed", "unknown", "larger", "smaller"]'::jsonb,3,'Less moment needs less material.','original'),
('127.409.d.12.F',3,'Shot peening strengthens a metal surface by creating compressive:','["residual stress", "sound", "paint layers", "color"]'::jsonb,0,'Compression resists crack growth.','original'),
('127.409.d.12.F',4,'Prestressed concrete uses steel tendons pulled tight to keep the concrete in:','["boredom", "compression", "motion", "tension"]'::jsonb,1,'Concrete is strong in compression.','original'),
('127.409.d.13.A',3,'When a simply supported beam bends downward under a center load, the top surface is in:','["vacuum", "torsion", "compression", "tension"]'::jsonb,2,'The top shortens.','original'),
('127.409.d.13.A',4,'In that same beam, the bottom surface is in:','["neutral state", "shear only", "compression", "tension"]'::jsonb,3,'The bottom stretches.','original'),
('127.409.d.13.B',3,'On the tension side of a bent beam, the material:','["elongates", "spins", "shortens", "vanishes"]'::jsonb,0,'Tension stretches material.','original'),
('127.409.d.13.B',4,'The line in a bent beam where the material neither stretches nor shortens is the:','["fracture line", "neutral axis", "power line", "yield line"]'::jsonb,1,'Strain is zero at the neutral axis.','original'),
('127.409.d.13.C',3,'A digital image correlation map of a bent beam shows positive strain on one side and negative strain on the other. The positive side is in:','["shear", "compression", "tension", "a vacuum"]'::jsonb,2,'Positive strain means stretching.','original'),
('127.409.d.13.C',4,'Color bands in a DIC strain map help an engineer see where strain is:','["named", "colored by paint", "printed", "concentrated"]'::jsonb,3,'Maps highlight strain hot spots.','original'),
('127.409.d.14.A',3,'Welding and brazing are examples of:','["permanent joints", "non-permanent joints", "adjustable joints", "hinge joints"]'::jsonb,0,'They fuse parts together.','original'),
('127.409.d.14.A',4,'A bolted joint is considered non-permanent because it can be:','["never opened", "taken apart without damage", "painted over", "melted"]'::jsonb,1,'Fasteners can be removed.','original'),
('127.409.d.14.B',3,'Tightening a bolt to a specified torque creates a preload, also called:','["post-stress", "random stress", "pre-stress", "no stress"]'::jsonb,2,'Preload clamps the joint.','original'),
('127.409.d.14.B',4,'A bolt rated for 8,000 N is used to carry 2,000 N. Its factor of safety is:','["a factor of 6", "a factor of 16,000", "a factor of 0.25", "a factor of 4"]'::jsonb,3,'8,000 / 2,000 = 4.','original'),
('127.409.d.14.C',3,'Designing a bracket to hold 500 N requires choosing a material and cross section so that the stress stays below the material''s:','["allowable strength", "market price", "brand name", "color rating"]'::jsonb,0,'Design stress must be within limits.','original'),
('127.409.d.14.C',4,'After designing a support structure, an engineer should verify the design by:','["naming it", "testing or calculating it against the load", "photographing it only", "painting it"]'::jsonb,1,'Verification confirms performance.','original'),
('127.410.d.1',3,'A structural engineer discovers that a balcony design she approved cannot carry the posted load. She should:','["wait for a failure", "blame the builder", "notify the owner and correct the design", "stay silent"]'::jsonb,2,'Public safety requires correcting errors.','original'),
('127.410.d.1',4,'Engineering codes of ethics require engineers to act within their:','["spare time", "favorite hobbies", "budget only", "areas of competence"]'::jsonb,3,'Engineers should only practice where qualified.','original'),
('127.410.d.2.A',3,'The Eiffel Tower, completed in 1889, is significant because it demonstrated the use of:','["wrought-iron lattice construction", "glass walls", "stone arches", "wooden beams"]'::jsonb,0,'It showed how open iron frames can be tall and strong.','original'),
('127.410.d.2.A',4,'The Brooklyn Bridge was a pioneering suspension bridge that used steel:','["domes", "cables", "arches", "rafters"]'::jsonb,1,'Steel wire cables carry the deck.','original'),
('127.410.d.2.B',3,'Ancient builders in regions with abundant timber often built with wood, while those near quarries built with:','["glass", "aluminum", "stone", "plastic"]'::jsonb,2,'Local materials shaped construction.','original'),
('127.410.d.2.B',4,'Adobe buildings are common in dry regions because the clay and straw needed are:','["illegal", "imported", "expensive only", "locally available"]'::jsonb,3,'Builders use materials at hand.','original'),
('127.410.d.2.C',3,'Archimedes is credited with explaining the principle of the:','["lever", "steam engine", "telephone", "transistor"]'::jsonb,0,'He described how levers multiply force.','original'),
('127.410.d.2.C',4,'Galileo studied the strength of beams and noted that scaling a structure up in size makes it relatively:','["cheaper", "weaker", "brighter", "stronger"]'::jsonb,1,'Weight grows faster than strength.','original'),
('127.410.d.2.D',3,'A structural engineer who designs buildings to resist loads uses the field of:','["astronomy", "geology only", "statics", "linguistics"]'::jsonb,2,'Statics analyzes forces in equilibrium.','original'),
('127.410.d.2.D',4,'Careers that use statics include civil engineer, architect, and:','["poet", "DJ", "pastry chef", "mechanical designer"]'::jsonb,3,'Many design careers rely on statics.','original'),
('127.410.d.3.A',3,'A beam measured as 96 inches long is how many feet long?','["8 feet", "12 feet", "9 feet", "80 feet"]'::jsonb,0,'96 / 12 = 8.','original'),
('127.410.d.3.A',4,'Which tool would best measure a beam''s length to the nearest 1/16 inch?','["bathroom scale", "tape measure with 1/16 inch marks", "clock", "thermometer"]'::jsonb,1,'Choose a tool with suitable resolution.','original'),
('127.410.d.3.B',3,'The prefix ''kilo'' means:','["one tenth of", "one hundredth of", "one thousand times", "one million times"]'::jsonb,2,'1 kilo = 1,000.','original'),
('127.410.d.3.B',4,'Convert 3.5 kilonewtons to newtons.','["35 N", "350 N", "35,000 N", "3,500 N"]'::jsonb,3,'3.5 x 1,000 = 3,500.','original'),
('127.410.d.3.C',3,'An apple weighs approximately:','["1 newton", "100 newtons", "1 meganewton", "1 kilonewton"]'::jsonb,0,'A 100 g apple weighs about 1 N.','original'),
('127.410.d.3.C',4,'A force of about 1 pound is closest to:','["450 newtons", "4.45 newtons", "45 newtons", "0.45 newtons"]'::jsonb,1,'1 lb is about 4.45 N.','original'),
('127.410.d.4.A',3,'A book presses down on a table with 10 N. By Newton''s third law, the table pushes up on the book with:','["a 20 N force", "no force", "an equal 10 N force", "a 5 N force"]'::jsonb,2,'Action and reaction are equal.','original'),
('127.410.d.4.A',4,'In a static system, every action force has a reaction force that is:','["larger and in the same direction", "smaller", "absent", "equal in size and opposite in direction"]'::jsonb,3,'That is Newton''s third law.','original'),
('127.410.d.4.B',3,'Gears are used to transmit motion and change:','["speed and torque", "sound only", "color and size", "price only"]'::jsonb,0,'Gear ratios trade speed for torque.','original'),
('127.410.d.4.B',4,'A pulley is used mainly to change the direction of a:','["magnet", "force applied by a rope", "gear tooth", "bolt"]'::jsonb,1,'Pulleys redirect cable force.','original'),
('127.410.d.4.C',3,'A gear with 40 teeth drives a gear with 10 teeth. The smaller gear turns:','["not at all", "at the same speed", "faster than the larger", "slower than the larger"]'::jsonb,2,'Fewer teeth means faster rotation.','original'),
('127.410.d.4.C',4,'A lever with the pivot closer to the load than to the effort gives a mechanical advantage that is:','["exactly 0", "negative", "less than 1", "greater than 1"]'::jsonb,3,'A longer effort arm multiplies force.','original'),
('127.410.d.4.D',3,'A uniform distributed load of 50 N/m acts over 6 m. The equivalent point load is:','["300 N", "3,000 N", "56 N", "8.3 N"]'::jsonb,0,'50 x 6 = 300.','original'),
('127.410.d.4.D',4,'The equivalent point load of a uniform distributed load acts at the:','["end of the beam", "midpoint of the loaded length", "edge of the paper", "support"]'::jsonb,1,'The resultant acts at the centroid.','original'),
('127.410.d.4.E',3,'A line load is measured in newtons per:','["volt", "kilogram", "meter", "second"]'::jsonb,2,'Line loads are force per length.','original'),
('127.410.d.4.E',4,'Pressure on a roof from snow is measured as force per:','["unit length", "unit mass", "unit time", "unit area"]'::jsonb,3,'Area loads are force per area.','original'),
('127.410.d.4.F',3,'Torque is commonly measured in:','["newton-meters", "watts", "newtons per meter", "kilograms"]'::jsonb,0,'Torque = force x distance.','original'),
('127.410.d.4.F',4,'A 20 N force applied 0.5 m from a pivot, perpendicular to the lever, produces a torque of:','["20.5 N-m", "10 N-m", "40 N-m", "0.025 N-m"]'::jsonb,1,'20 x 0.5 = 10.','original'),
('127.410.d.4.G',3,'Efficiency of a machine is the ratio of useful output work to:','["output sound", "machine price", "input work", "machine weight"]'::jsonb,2,'Efficiency = output / input.','original'),
('127.410.d.4.G',4,'A machine takes in 500 J and delivers 400 J of useful work. Its efficiency is:','["125 percent", "400 percent", "20 percent", "80 percent"]'::jsonb,3,'400 / 500 = 0.8.','original'),
('127.410.d.4.H',3,'A couple consists of two forces that are equal, opposite, and:','["parallel but not along the same line", "zero", "along the same line", "perpendicular"]'::jsonb,0,'Offset parallel forces create rotation.','original')
