('127.410.d.5.H',3,'Varignon''s theorem says the moment of a force about a point equals the sum of the moments of its:','["units", "labels", "colors", "components"]'::jsonb,3,'Component moments add to the total.','original'),
('127.410.d.5.H',4,'Using components can simplify finding a moment because each component''s perpendicular distance is often:','["easier to determine", "impossible to find", "unrelated", "always zero"]'::jsonb,0,'Components align with axes.','original'),
('127.410.d.5.I',3,'A force can be moved to a different point on a body if a corresponding moment is added. This creates a:','["unit set", "force-couple system", "color scheme", "price list"]'::jsonb,1,'Equivalent systems simplify analysis.','original'),
('127.410.d.5.I',4,'Replacing several loads on a beam with one equivalent force and one couple makes the beam easier to:','["paint", "sell", "analyze", "name"]'::jsonb,2,'Equivalent loads simplify calculations.','original'),
('127.410.d.6.A',3,'The centroid of an object is its geometric center, while the center of mass is the point where its:','["color changes", "price is set", "surface ends", "mass is balanced"]'::jsonb,3,'Center of mass accounts for how mass is distributed.','original'),
('127.410.d.6.A',4,'For an object of uniform density, the centroid and the center of mass are:','["at the same location", "always far apart", "outside the object", "undefined"]'::jsonb,0,'Uniform density makes them coincide.','original'),
('127.410.d.6.B',3,'The centroid of a rectangle that is 8 cm wide and 6 cm tall, measured from its lower left corner, is at:','["(3 cm, 4 cm)", "(4 cm, 3 cm)", "(8 cm, 6 cm)", "(0 cm, 0 cm)"]'::jsonb,1,'Half the width and half the height.','original'),
('127.410.d.6.B',4,'To find the centroid of a complex shape, an engineer often splits it into simple shapes and uses a:','["price list", "random guess", "weighted average of their centroids", "color chart"]'::jsonb,2,'Area-weighted averaging locates the combined centroid.','original'),
('127.410.d.6.C',3,'The balance point of a uniform solid sphere is found at its:','["surface", "top", "bottom", "center"]'::jsonb,3,'A symmetric uniform body balances at its center.','original'),
('127.410.d.6.C',4,'A flat uniform plate is hung from one corner and then another. The center of mass lies where the plumb lines:','["cross", "end", "stay parallel", "start"]'::jsonb,0,'The intersection marks the balance point.','original'),
('127.410.d.7.A',3,'A lever rotates about a fixed point called the:','["chain", "pivot", "bolt head", "gear"]'::jsonb,1,'A pivot is the point of rotation.','original'),
('127.410.d.7.A',4,'A 30 N child sits 2 m from the pivot of a seesaw. A second child on the other side balances it at 1 m. The second child weighs:','["30 N", "15 N", "60 N", "90 N"]'::jsonb,2,'30 x 2 = W x 1, so W = 60.','original'),
('127.410.d.7.B',3,'A ladder leaning on a wall with only friction at the floor stays put when friction is:','["not present", "less than the push", "zero", "large enough to balance the horizontal push"]'::jsonb,3,'Static friction must supply the balancing force.','original'),
('127.410.d.7.B',4,'A smoother floor lowers friction, making a leaning ladder more likely to:','["slip", "grow", "stick harder", "float"]'::jsonb,0,'Less friction allows sliding.','original'),
('127.410.d.7.C',3,'A person standing on the ladder adds a downward force. To stay stable, the floor friction must now be:','["unchanged in all cases", "greater", "smaller", "absent"]'::jsonb,1,'More load requires more friction to hold.','original'),
('127.410.d.7.C',4,'A crate is pushed with a force less than the maximum static friction. The crate:','["speeds up", "accelerates", "stays at rest", "tips up instantly"]'::jsonb,2,'The friction force matches the push until its limit.','original'),
('127.410.d.7.D',3,'The maximum static friction force equals the coefficient of static friction times the:','["color", "volume", "speed", "normal force"]'::jsonb,3,'f = mu x N.','original'),
('127.410.d.7.D',4,'A 100 N block sits on a surface with a static friction coefficient of 0.4. The maximum static friction is:','["40 N", "140 N", "0.4 N", "250 N"]'::jsonb,0,'0.4 x 100 = 40.','original'),
('127.410.d.8.A',3,'A support that allows rotation but prevents movement in both horizontal and vertical directions is called a:','["roller support", "pin support", "free end", "spring"]'::jsonb,1,'A pin gives two reaction forces.','original'),
('127.410.d.8.A',4,'A support that resists horizontal and vertical force and also rotation is a:','["roller support", "pin support", "fixed support", "free end"]'::jsonb,2,'A fixed support provides two forces and a moment.','original'),
('127.410.d.8.B',3,'A roller support provides how many reaction forces?','["none", "two", "three", "one"]'::jsonb,3,'It resists force only perpendicular to its surface.','original'),
('127.410.d.8.B',4,'A planar fixed support restricts how many degrees of freedom?','["three", "zero", "one", "two"]'::jsonb,0,'Two translations and one rotation.','original'),
('127.410.d.8.C',3,'A bridge is built with a pin at one end and a roller at the other so that the bridge can:','["float", "expand and contract with temperature", "spin", "vanish"]'::jsonb,1,'The roller allows thermal movement.','original'),
('127.410.d.8.C',4,'Replacing a roller with a pin on a long bridge could cause large stresses when it heats because the bridge:','["gets lighter", "loses color", "cannot lengthen freely", "turns invisible"]'::jsonb,2,'Restrained expansion creates force.','original'),
('127.410.d.8.D',3,'A fixed support at the wall end of a cantilever beam provides a reaction moment to resist the beam''s tendency to:','["melt", "float", "shrink", "rotate"]'::jsonb,3,'The moment prevents rotation.','original'),
('127.410.d.8.D',4,'A beam on a pin and a roller has how many unknown reaction components in a plane?','["three", "one", "six", "two"]'::jsonb,0,'Two from the pin and one from the roller.','original'),
('127.410.d.9.A',3,'A complete free-body diagram includes the object and all the:','["nearby words", "external forces and moments on it", "internal colors", "unrelated objects"]'::jsonb,1,'FBDs show all outside influences.','original'),
('127.410.d.9.A',4,'When drawing a free-body diagram, a support is replaced by its:','["paint", "name", "reaction forces", "price"]'::jsonb,2,'Supports are shown as reactions.','original'),
('127.410.d.9.B',3,'An object in static equilibrium has a net force of:','["one newton", "ten newtons", "infinity", "zero"]'::jsonb,3,'Equilibrium means no net force or moment.','original'),
('127.410.d.9.B',4,'Besides zero net force, static equilibrium requires zero net:','["moment", "volume", "price", "color"]'::jsonb,0,'Both translation and rotation must be balanced.','original'),
('127.410.d.9.C',3,'In two dimensions, the translational equilibrium equations are the sum of forces in x equal to zero and the sum of forces in:','["time equal to zero", "y equal to zero", "x equal to ten", "z equal to ten"]'::jsonb,1,'Two force equations in a plane.','original'),
