('127.410.d.9.D',3,'A beam is supported at both ends and carries a 100 N load at its center. Each support carries:','["25 N", "100 N", "200 N", "50 N"]'::jsonb,3,'By symmetry, 100 / 2 = 50.','original'),
('127.410.d.9.D',4,'A 10 m beam on supports at its ends carries a 300 N load 2 m from the left end. The right support reaction is:','["60 N", "30 N", "150 N", "240 N"]'::jsonb,0,'Moments about left: R x 10 = 300 x 2, so R = 60.','original'),
('127.410.d.10.A',3,'In a truss, each member is generally loaded only in:','["bending only", "tension or compression", "sound only", "twisting only"]'::jsonb,1,'Two-force members carry axial loads.','original'),
('127.410.d.10.A',4,'A plane truss made of triangles is stable because a triangle cannot change its shape without changing the:','["name", "color", "length of a side", "price"]'::jsonb,2,'Triangles are rigid.','original'),
('127.410.d.10.B',3,'The method of joints solves for member forces by applying equilibrium to each:','["paint layer", "weld color", "label", "connection point"]'::jsonb,3,'Joint equilibrium gives member forces.','original'),
('127.410.d.10.B',4,'The method of sections finds member forces by cutting the truss and applying equilibrium to:','["one side of the cut", "no part", "the paint", "the whole planet"]'::jsonb,0,'A section isolates the cut members.','original'),
